  "strategy": "Inspired by the Euclidean to the Park's theorem and quasi-convex optimization, I propose a unique approach that combines random phase perturbations with adaptive spectral decomposition. This method aims to overcome the plateau effect observed in local search techniques (like LP-only alternation) by introducing coordinate-wise impulse adjustments and focusing on peak reduction through a novel frequency isolation technique.\n\n### Approach and Rationale\n1. **Rooting**: We'll start by initializing the search with a fallback sequence of length 200 (all elements set to 1).\n2. **Mismatch Correction**: If a parent sequence is available that gives a lower objective, clamp and normalize it for processing.\n3. **Quasi-convex Local Descent**: For each current best candidate, we'll alternate between two key operations:\n   - **Gradient Trimming**: Use a subgradient-based method to focus on specific peak-reducing constriction.\n   - **Spectral Refinement**: Extract dominant frequencies from the autocorrelation to guide coefficient adjustments.\n4. **Independent Update Strategy**: The primary innovation lies in separating peak-specific recovery from generalized optimization. For each iteration, we'll carefully choose coefficients for impact moves (initially focusing on the central fourth of the sequence) and then progressively widen this target area.\n5. **Length-resampling Breakout**: If the local search reaches a plateau, we'll use uniform interpolation to generate new sequence lengths within a bounded range.\n\n### Mathematical Specification\nLet \\(a = (a_0, a_1, \\dots, a_{n-1})\\) be our sequence candidate.\n\n**Objective**:\n\\[ \\min_{a} \\frac{2n \\cdot \\max\\left(\\sum_{k}|a_k a_{\\{j-k\\}^+}| \\right)}{(\\sum_j a_j)^2} \\]\n\n**Constraints**:\n\\[ 0 \\leq a_i \\leq 1000 \\quad \\forall i \\]\n\n**Key Operations**:\n1. **Convolution Projection**:\n   \\[ \\text{proj}_{(i,j)}(a) = \\min_{t} \\left\\{ a_i, a_j \\right\\} \\cdot \\left(1 + \\text{peak\\_rate}_{i,j}\\right) \\]\n   which reduces the impact of autocorrelation at positions \\(i+j\\).\n\n2. **Frequency Isolation**:\n   \\[ f_k = \\left(\\text{conv}(a, a)_k\\right)^{\\alpha} / \\sum_{j} \\left(\\text{conv}(a, a)_j\\right)^{\\alpha} \\]\n   using a soft-thresholding parameter \\(\\alpha = 1.2\\) (guaranteed by a reference-time scaling factor).\n\n### Implementation Procedure\n1. **Initialization**:\n   - Start with sequence `best` from fallback (length 200, sums normalized to 1).\n   - Compute initial score: \\( \\text{score}_0 = \\frac{2 \\cdot n \\cdot \\max(\\text{conv}(best, best))}{(\\sum_j best_j)^2} \\)\n\n2. **Iteration Setup**:\n   - Allocate 50 seconds for gradient trimming and 100 seconds for spectral refinement per cycle.\n   - Set trigger parameters:\n     \\[ \\tau = 0.6 \\text{ of the initial peak value} \\]\n     \\[ \\mu = 0.9 \\text{ momentum decay over iterations} \\]\n\n3. **Cycle Execution**:\n   - For Phase 1 (auto-optimization): Perform 10 gradient trimming steps with adaptive step sizes, focusing on the central indices first.\n   - For Phase 2 (peak reduction): Invoke soft-frequency alignment, biasing away from high-impact coefficients.\n\n4. **Plateau Detection**:\n   - If no \\( 0.3\\%\\text{ improvement for } 2 \\text{ consecutive cycles} \\):\n     - Adjust sequence length by \\(\\pm 25\\%\\).\n     - Generate a generic lookup table of sequence candidates for this new length.\n\n5. **Fallback Handling**:\n   - If main processes fail, return a sequence of length 400 with uniform values (sum=199).0.\n\n### Search Choices\n1. **Length Range**: Primarily target sequences from 100 to 800 coefficients.\n2. **Time Budget**: Use exponential time scaling with base budget 7.5 seconds per optimization step.\n3. **Coordinate Prioritization**: Initially focus on central 40% (improves peak reduction consistency).\n4. **Gradient Correction**: Apply coordinate-wise impulses (gamma-law normalization with exponent 1.37).\n\nThis implementation explicitly avoids the previous gradient-only solutions by incorporating both bound preservation and frequency-enhanced random restarts, resulting in a more globally optimal search process.",
